1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
3,000 pounds of race car out of control.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:06,000
You're going to lose it.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:07,000
You're going to crash.

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:08,000
You're going to spin.

5
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Motorcycles crumple in high-speed smash-ups.

6
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:15,000
A woman hits the Earth from over 10,000 feet

7
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
with a failed parachute.

8
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
I was certain I was going to die from that impact.

9
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
From colliding asteroids to subatomic particles,

10
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, crashes on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
High speeds.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Close formations.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Split-second maneuvers.

14
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:55,900
Yellow fly, yellow fly, yellow.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,800
All the things that make auto racing so thrilling

16
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:01,940
also make it deadly.

17
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,540
Actor-turned-race car driver Frankie Muniz

18
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:07,620
knows just how dangerous the sport can be.

19
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:09,020
Being a race car driver, you know you're

20
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:11,380
going to be involved in accidents every once in a while,

21
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:12,880
big crashes and stuff.

22
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:14,620
It's kind of something that comes with the territory

23
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:15,860
of being a driver.

24
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:17,420
You know it's going to happen, and most of the time

25
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:20,660
you can't avoid it, and they hurt.

26
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:23,900
Last season, I actually fractured my back,

27
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,900
broke my wrist, broke my ankle, broke six ribs.

28
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,160
So I've had a few big ones.

29
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,300
One of the primary causes for auto racing crashes

30
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,420
is driver error.

31
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,320
The driver's always trying to find the limit on the car.

32
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:42,220
How far can I brake?

33
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,720
How fast can I take this corner?

34
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:46,360
Can I go a little bit faster?

35
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:49,060
And sometimes you just take it a little bit too fast,

36
00:01:49,061 --> 00:01:51,060
And the car just doesn't have the grip,

37
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:54,300
can't take that speed, and you will lose control of the car

38
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:55,600
and hit the wall.

39
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,000
On average, there's one serious injury

40
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,600
for every 300 racing accidents.

41
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:06,020
But 40 years ago, that ratio was 1 in 10.

42
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:12,020
When I started IndyCar racing in the late 60s,

43
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:14,320
we lost a guy a weekend.

44
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:16,520
There's a lot of fire deaths.

45
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:19,060
There are a lot of deaths from Sherman to the city.

46
00:02:19,061 --> 00:02:21,400
injuries and head injuries and that kind of stuff.

47
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:25,160
Basically, it was a tube-framed car with a 70-gallon fuel tank

48
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:28,000
on the back, and the driver just hung out in the open.

49
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,000
There was nothing to protect him.

50
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:32,800
Crashes have been part of auto racing

51
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:36,120
since its earliest days.

52
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:40,620
In 1911, the very first Indy 500 had a multi-car pileup

53
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,320
just 13 laps into the race.

54
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:49,060
As cars got faster, dangers increased.

55
00:02:49,061 --> 00:02:52,860
Early on in motorsports, the focus was make the car fast,

56
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:57,360
then make it reliable, and then worry about safety afterward.

57
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:01,100
The 1958 Indy race saw one of its largest accidents.

58
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:02,900
Car number five, the zinc special,

59
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,500
is the first direct, another over the wall.

60
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:09,220
And we've got one, two, three, four, five, six cars piled up

61
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:12,020
here on the northeast turn.

62
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:15,020
The fatal flip of driver Pat O'Connor's car

63
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:18,020
led to mandated metal roll bars and medically certified

64
00:03:18,021 --> 00:03:27,020
helmets for Indy drivers. The fire threat diminished in

65
00:03:27,021 --> 00:03:31,060
the 1970s when racing took a cue from the US military.

66
00:03:31,061 --> 00:03:34,060
They brought a helicopter fuel cell to Indianapolis

67
00:03:34,061 --> 00:03:36,980
Speedway and said, look, you can shoot a gun into this thing,

68
00:03:36,981 --> 00:03:39,980
you can hit it with an axe, you can do whatever you want, and

69
00:03:39,981 --> 00:03:41,980
it won't penetrate it. So we started running those in our

70
00:03:41,981 --> 00:03:46,980
cars. That went away. There was no more deaths from fire.

71
00:03:46,981 --> 00:03:50,480
In the 1980s, carbon fiber replaced steel plates

72
00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:54,520
and aluminum tubing in race car construction.

73
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:57,520
The result was a lighter yet stronger vehicle,

74
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:00,720
capable of deflecting energy away from the driver.

75
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:03,940
So when you crash, everything disintegrates.

76
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:07,540
All these suspension parts, the wheels, the front wing,

77
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:11,440
everything is designed to break off the car fairly easily.

78
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:15,240
And that way, the impact is it's absorbed by the car,

79
00:04:15,241 --> 00:04:17,540
not absorbed by the driver.

80
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:19,180
Here's the math.

81
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:23,180
A car in motion builds up kinetic energy.

82
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:25,680
The heavier the car and the faster it goes,

83
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:29,680
the more the kinetic energy increases.

84
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:33,800
A 3,600-pound racing car traveling at 180 miles per hour

85
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:37,300
has enough kinetic energy to launch an average man five

86
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,000
miles into the air.

87
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:43,400
If that race car runs head-on into a concrete barrier,

88
00:04:43,401 --> 00:04:46,040
All the kinetic energy is released in the milliseconds

89
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:48,140
it takes for the vehicle to stop,

90
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:51,040
with all of the force being absorbed by the car and driver.

91
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:05,380
A crash in which a car spins, tumbles, and shatters

92
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:08,520
releases the kinetic energy over a greater period of time,

93
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:09,520
dispersing the force.

94
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:15,400
Whenever there's a crash on a racetrack,

95
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,840
it's the job of the safety team to respond.

96
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:21,040
These specially trained paramedic firefighters

97
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:23,840
can be on the scene of an accident within 15 to 30

98
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:27,640
seconds, offering life-saving medical support

99
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:30,620
and vehicle extrication.

100
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:33,060
Vehicle extrication is most easily defined

101
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:35,820
as removing the vehicle from around the patient

102
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:38,460
as opposed to removing the patient from the vehicle.

103
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:41,760
And what we will do is we will cut the car apart around him

104
00:05:41,761 --> 00:05:45,100
around him so that we can limit the movement to the driver

105
00:05:45,101 --> 00:05:49,360
and minimize any further injury to the driver to get him out.

106
00:05:49,361 --> 00:05:51,800
In the event we have to cut a driver from the car

107
00:05:51,801 --> 00:05:54,740
because he's caught inside the cockpit,

108
00:05:54,741 --> 00:05:59,280
we can utilize one of our Almatro rescue tools.

109
00:05:59,281 --> 00:06:01,020
This is a combination tool.

110
00:06:01,021 --> 00:06:03,540
We can either spread the carbon fiber or cut it.

111
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:07,580
The most frequent injury that we've seen

112
00:06:07,581 --> 00:06:09,980
has probably been orthopedic injuries,

113
00:06:09,981 --> 00:06:11,460
arms and legs and things.

114
00:06:11,461 --> 00:06:16,760
We've done such a good job of safety with the new Hans devices

115
00:06:16,761 --> 00:06:18,740
and eliminating the head and neck movement

116
00:06:18,741 --> 00:06:21,060
that we don't see nearly as many of those injuries

117
00:06:21,061 --> 00:06:23,400
as we used to.

118
00:06:23,401 --> 00:06:26,580
The Hans, or head and neck support device,

119
00:06:26,581 --> 00:06:29,640
was designed by Dr. Robert Hubbard in the 1980s

120
00:06:29,641 --> 00:06:31,920
in response to the death of a friend who suffered head

121
00:06:31,921 --> 00:06:35,620
and neck injuries in an auto racing crash.

122
00:06:35,621 --> 00:06:38,620
Use of the Hans device was initially voluntary.

123
00:06:38,621 --> 00:06:41,220
It was not widely employed, but now

124
00:06:41,221 --> 00:06:43,980
following the death of racing giant Dale Earnhardt, Sr.

125
00:06:43,981 --> 00:06:46,220
in February 2001.

126
00:06:46,221 --> 00:06:48,100
Nearly every major race-sanctioning body

127
00:06:48,101 --> 00:06:50,800
requires its use.

128
00:06:50,801 --> 00:06:53,040
That was the biggest problem when you crashed.

129
00:06:53,041 --> 00:06:54,960
Your neck and your head was the only thing

130
00:06:54,961 --> 00:06:57,340
that was loose on the carcass, because you were tightened

131
00:06:57,341 --> 00:06:58,380
by so many seat belts.

132
00:06:58,381 --> 00:07:01,140
Every time you crashed, you could break your neck

133
00:07:01,141 --> 00:07:04,340
if you went forward, if you went sideways.

134
00:07:04,341 --> 00:07:08,140
It's actually a very simple safety device

135
00:07:08,141 --> 00:07:11,640
that will prevent the driver from injuring their neck.

136
00:07:11,641 --> 00:07:14,640
So the seatbelt goes on top of this hands device

137
00:07:14,641 --> 00:07:16,140
where my hands are.

138
00:07:16,141 --> 00:07:18,280
So in case there's an accident,

139
00:07:18,281 --> 00:07:20,160
you see, this is as far as my neck goes,

140
00:07:20,161 --> 00:07:22,420
because the hands device immediately

141
00:07:22,421 --> 00:07:24,160
grabs the helmet from over here.

142
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,620
I have had hard crashes at over 200 miles an hour,

143
00:07:28,621 --> 00:07:30,120
and thanks to the hands device,

144
00:07:30,121 --> 00:07:32,160
nothing has happened to my neck.

145
00:07:32,161 --> 00:07:34,160
And it's, which is funny, when they introduced it,

146
00:07:34,161 --> 00:07:35,160
drivers didn't want to wear it,

147
00:07:35,161 --> 00:07:37,340
because they thought it was uncomfortable.

148
00:07:37,341 --> 00:07:40,380
And now, you would never think of driving without the hand

149
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:41,480
device.

150
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:44,880
It would be like almost driving without wheels on your car.

151
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:46,480
Even more lives might be saved

152
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:48,580
by the adoption of another breakthrough in motorsport

153
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:53,460
engineering, safer barriers, standing for steel and foam

154
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:54,820
energy reduction barriers.

155
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:01,060
As this test footage shows, the vehicle

156
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:03,800
slams into the barrier with tremendous force,

157
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:05,760
but the wall compresses, absorbing

158
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:06,760
the energy of the collision.

159
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:11,500
The outer layer closest to the track

160
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:15,140
is made of hollow 8-inch steel tubes, which are both strong

161
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:18,740
and flexible during an impact.

162
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:21,940
The center layer is dense 22-inch thick polystyrene

163
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:26,480
foam, which absorbs energy by compressing.

164
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:28,580
The inner layer nearest the spectators

165
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:32,380
is solid concrete to provide resistance.

166
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:35,060
With soft walls, there's a certain amount of elasticity

167
00:08:35,061 --> 00:08:37,460
and give, and as a consequence of that,

168
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,600
why the driver has a fairly good chance

169
00:08:39,700 --> 00:08:42,300
of being able to survive a fairly strong impact

170
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:46,700
against those walls after a collision has occurred.

171
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:48,800
Race cars may avoid even more crashes,

172
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:53,420
thanks to revolutionary changes in racing electronics.

173
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:55,220
Here at the Panther Racing Team facility

174
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:58,220
in Indianapolis, Indiana, the crew prepares their car

175
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:00,920
for the Indy 500.

176
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:03,620
They're installing communication systems that didn't exist

177
00:09:03,621 --> 00:09:07,620
10 years ago. Not only on this car,

178
00:09:07,621 --> 00:09:10,660
but on all 33 cars participating in this year's race, there are

179
00:09:10,661 --> 00:09:14,660
several safety components that are mandatory for each car.

180
00:09:14,661 --> 00:09:17,540
The familiar yellow caution flag that warns drivers of an

181
00:09:17,541 --> 00:09:21,540
accident on the racetrack is now augmented by an onboard signal

182
00:09:21,541 --> 00:09:24,740
sent electronically by race officials.

183
00:09:24,741 --> 00:09:26,580
This is our track condition radio.

184
00:09:26,581 --> 00:09:29,380
It receives messages from race control when there's a caution or

185
00:09:29,381 --> 00:09:33,620
yellow flag on the track. This box is located here on the

186
00:09:33,621 --> 00:09:36,620
and receives a signal from the antenna link here,

187
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:40,960
and then drives two LEDs on here at this location

188
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:43,260
to alert the driver that there's been a caution.

189
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:47,060
It comes on very quickly, and traveling at 200 miles an hour,

190
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:49,360
you're traveling almost a football field a second,

191
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:51,440
and any early warning you can get

192
00:09:51,540 --> 00:09:55,080
will help you avoid contact with another car.

193
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:58,020
The team also installs a data acquisition recorder

194
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:00,180
in case a crash occurs.

195
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:02,580
This helps us get information from crashes

196
00:10:02,581 --> 00:10:06,180
on what happens to the driver and the car during an accident.

197
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:10,480
This is very similar to a crash box on an airplane.

198
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:13,120
60 sensors on the car gather information,

199
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:15,820
ranging from brake pressure and g-forces

200
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:20,040
to steering inputs and ride height changes.

201
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:22,540
The accident data recorder not only receives data

202
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,540
from sensors located on the car, but it also receives data

203
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:27,840
from sensors located in the driver's ears.

204
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:31,400
These earpieces, which also provide communication

205
00:10:31,401 --> 00:10:35,160
communication to the team provide us with G-load data

206
00:10:35,161 --> 00:10:38,440
about what happens to the driver's head during an accident.

207
00:10:38,441 --> 00:10:42,280
After a crash, G-force data from the driver's earpieces

208
00:10:42,281 --> 00:10:46,600
are downloaded from the accident data recorder and analyzed.

209
00:10:46,601 --> 00:10:51,480
During the analysis, we're looking for, one, how much force the driver

210
00:10:51,481 --> 00:10:54,600
experienced or how much injury he might have sustained in the crash

211
00:10:54,601 --> 00:10:59,640
based on acceleration, and then two, if there's anything that can be done to the

212
00:10:59,641 --> 00:11:02,600
car to make it safer in the future.

213
00:11:02,601 --> 00:11:05,180
This is a crash from two years ago at Indy.

214
00:11:05,181 --> 00:11:07,920
You can see turns one and two down the front stretch, turn

215
00:11:07,921 --> 00:11:11,100
three, and then the driver crashes going into turn four.

216
00:11:11,101 --> 00:11:13,600
You can see the first spike here where the car actually

217
00:11:13,601 --> 00:11:14,580
breaks loose.

218
00:11:14,581 --> 00:11:16,820
At this point here, the car has lost traction

219
00:11:16,821 --> 00:11:18,360
and starts to slide.

220
00:11:18,361 --> 00:11:21,700
Slides across the track, and here he hits the wall.

221
00:11:21,701 --> 00:11:24,900
The driver's head saw approximately 80 Gs,

222
00:11:24,901 --> 00:11:27,360
and the car saw about 150 Gs.

223
00:11:27,361 --> 00:11:32,740
As data is analyzed and you look at the injuries and the performance of a car in a crash,

224
00:11:32,860 --> 00:11:37,000
and they make improvements for the cars to make them safer, the trend is to see fewer injuries.

225
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:43,820
I think the next step for us from a safety standpoint is not just to get data from a driver's earpieces,

226
00:11:43,821 --> 00:11:49,140
but we're going to also start putting sensors on the seatbelts and get data from the loads

227
00:11:49,141 --> 00:11:52,900
that the driver experiences during the accident with respect to his torso.

228
00:11:53,540 --> 00:11:54,900
Sensors are going to get better.

229
00:11:54,901 --> 00:11:58,100
We may be able to monitor heart rate and respiration rate

230
00:11:58,200 --> 00:11:59,800
on the drivers during the race.

231
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:01,900
And that's what's going to make the whole thing safer

232
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,900
for everybody.

233
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,940
While crashes in auto racing are becoming safer,

234
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:11,140
another motor sport is becoming more dangerous,

235
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:14,660
riding motorcycles.

236
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:18,560
In 1955, a race car in Le Mans, France,

237
00:12:18,660 --> 00:12:22,300
crashed, killing the driver and over 80 spectators.

238
00:12:22,301 --> 00:12:25,300
It is racing's deadliest accident.

239
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:27,800
Crashes will return on Modern Marvels.

240
00:12:39,960 --> 00:12:42,460
There are over 6 million motorcycles on the road

241
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:47,160
in the United States. 100,000 riders are involved

242
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:52,300
in crashes each year. And 5,000 of those are fatal.

243
00:12:52,301 --> 00:12:54,840
I'd say it's risky.

244
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:58,200
Statistics would show it's about, oh, maybe 25 to 30

245
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:01,040
times more dangerous, if you want to use that word,

246
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:03,440
than car driving.

247
00:13:03,540 --> 00:13:06,920
Deadly motorcycle crashes doubled in the last 10 years,

248
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:09,980
and the numbers continue to rise due to motorcycle automobile

249
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:11,820
collisions.

250
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:14,420
Three-fourths of the crashes occurred at intersections

251
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:17,660
in a motorcycle car crash, and two-thirds of that time,

252
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:20,560
it was a car driver violating the right-of-way of the motorcyclist.

253
00:13:20,561 --> 00:13:23,060
If it's an intersection crash, a car driver usually

254
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:24,260
doesn't see the motorcyclist.

255
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:30,600
Understanding the mechanics of motorcycle crashes

256
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:34,040
is the business of exponent failure analysis.

257
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:36,840
In general, we hope to learn specifics about how a vehicle

258
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:40,220
performs in a crash, both how it crushes, how it absorbs energy,

259
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:43,620
how it cushions the occupants and protects them in the crash,

260
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:46,660
and also how the occupants perform.

261
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:50,560
We do this for insurance carriers.

262
00:13:50,561 --> 00:13:54,000
motorcycle manufacturers, and then we do a lot of litigation

263
00:13:54,100 --> 00:13:56,300
related work.

264
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:59,060
Exponent Failure Analysis conducts crash tests

265
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:04,500
at its 147-acre research facility in Phoenix, Arizona.

266
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:07,140
Today we're going to be testing some motorcycle impact

267
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:10,680
mechanics, a motorcycle hitting a passenger vehicle.

268
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:12,780
The motorcycle in this test will

269
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:16,280
be going down the rail at approximately 30 miles an hour.

270
00:14:16,380 --> 00:14:19,360
And we've got a car that's going to be coming across from our 90

271
00:14:19,361 --> 00:14:20,360
road.

272
00:14:20,460 --> 00:14:23,860
It's going to be coming in at 15 miles an hour.

273
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:26,860
A half mile long wire cable accelerates the vehicles

274
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:29,200
to impact speed.

275
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:32,000
We're in the engine room where we have two large block Ford V8

276
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:34,900
engines in series to drive the cables that move the cars for

277
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,120
the crash test.

278
00:14:36,220 --> 00:14:38,120
That drives the cable systems.

279
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:41,120
The two cable systems are interlocked by two heavy truck

280
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:44,720
transmissions that give us about 100 different gear ratios for

281
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:46,720
controlling the relative speeds of the two vehicles that hit

282
00:14:46,820 --> 00:14:49,020
each other.

283
00:14:49,021 --> 00:14:52,160
The Exponent team painstakingly prepares the car,

284
00:14:52,260 --> 00:14:54,660
the motorcycle, crash test dummy,

285
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:56,760
and the seven cameras that will record the impact.

286
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:03,560
This is the onboard Dolly camera, which will be traveling down

287
00:15:03,660 --> 00:15:05,160
with the motorcycle.

288
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:07,580
And I have to set the aperture and make sure that it's set

289
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:11,380
for the 500 frames a second that we'll be recording it at.

290
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:13,520
Photo, you guys ready?

291
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:15,320
Standing back.

292
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:18,520
You shoot from the side to get the pitch motion

293
00:15:18,521 --> 00:15:19,760
of the motorcycle.

294
00:15:19,761 --> 00:15:21,860
The boom's going up.

295
00:15:21,861 --> 00:15:23,560
You shoot from the top so you can study

296
00:15:23,561 --> 00:15:27,260
how much it rotates on impact.

297
00:15:27,261 --> 00:15:28,760
And then you shoot from the front

298
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:33,160
so you can study where the occupant comes over the back.

299
00:15:33,161 --> 00:15:34,760
Hector, you ready?

300
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:42,580
Roll out.

301
00:15:42,581 --> 00:15:43,580
She's all yours.

302
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:50,760
the throttle up.

303
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:53,400
15 feet before impact, the vehicles

304
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:56,220
are released from the cable and hurtled towards one another

305
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:57,320
with their own momentum.

306
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:02,600
Perfect.

307
00:16:02,700 --> 00:16:03,440
Good hit.

308
00:16:03,540 --> 00:16:05,600
See how the dummy came off?

309
00:16:05,700 --> 00:16:06,440
Yep.

310
00:16:06,540 --> 00:16:08,400
It's all over in the blink of an eye,

311
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:12,580
but the high-speed cameras reveal much more.

312
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,740
The initial contact with the car, what happens

313
00:16:14,840 --> 00:16:17,880
is the front tire starts to engage the car.

314
00:16:17,881 --> 00:16:21,020
At that point, the forks start to rotate.

315
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:22,680
And once you hit the steering limit,

316
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:24,420
that's where you get some twist in the forks.

317
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:26,560
And at the same time, the bike is still coming in,

318
00:16:26,660 --> 00:16:28,920
compressing the forks against the car.

319
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:31,220
And that's where everything gets folded back.

320
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:34,200
You've got solid contact.

321
00:16:34,300 --> 00:16:37,840
Exponent's researchers survey the wreckage.

322
00:16:37,940 --> 00:16:40,740
So the front wheel impacts here in the door.

323
00:16:40,840 --> 00:16:42,240
Bike spins around.

324
00:16:42,340 --> 00:16:45,940
The axle's hitting right here, or the forks.

325
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:47,340
Dummy hits up here.

326
00:16:47,341 --> 00:16:56,460
Then you can see the same damage you can see on the front of the helmet where the dummy's helmet came into contact with the car.

327
00:16:57,300 --> 00:16:58,000
Right here.

328
00:16:58,660 --> 00:17:04,620
We've got a central strike here from the dummy's pelvis denting in the tank.

329
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:11,140
And the bike also rotated out from under the dummy, so he also has a strike on the one side of the bike.

330
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:16,040
As the side of the bike presented itself, his pelvis continued and dented in here.

331
00:17:16,041 --> 00:17:20,780
We'll be able to see that better on the high-speed video.

332
00:17:20,781 --> 00:17:23,580
The 500 frames per second video slows the action

333
00:17:23,581 --> 00:17:26,880
by more than 16 times.

334
00:17:26,881 --> 00:17:30,520
As the wheel engages, the bike slows down rapidly,

335
00:17:30,521 --> 00:17:34,220
and the motorcyclist keeps going at the same speed.

336
00:17:34,221 --> 00:17:38,800
The rider's pelvis comes into contact with the tank.

337
00:17:38,801 --> 00:17:41,860
And then right after that, we get a glancing head contact

338
00:17:41,861 --> 00:17:44,940
or helmet contact with the rear corner of the vehicle.

339
00:17:44,941 --> 00:17:49,080
probably would not have caused any significant head injury.

340
00:17:49,180 --> 00:17:51,280
But I wouldn't sell this short.

341
00:17:51,380 --> 00:17:55,980
There is injury potential here because of that pelvic.

342
00:17:56,080 --> 00:17:57,880
In addition to the video evidence,

343
00:17:57,980 --> 00:18:00,480
Christine will consult the data from dozens of sensors

344
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:02,700
mounted on the dummy.

345
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:05,400
He's built with lots of instrumentation

346
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:09,900
built in in his head, his neck, his chest, his leg,

347
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,740
as we see here, to measure the forces in the accident

348
00:18:13,741 --> 00:18:15,780
and tell us whether an injury may have occurred

349
00:18:15,781 --> 00:18:16,900
in that accident.

350
00:18:16,901 --> 00:18:19,300
This is the chest cavity and the rib cage of the dummy.

351
00:18:19,301 --> 00:18:21,340
We removed the front structure or sternum.

352
00:18:21,341 --> 00:18:23,980
So you can see there's a computer or data acquisition

353
00:18:23,981 --> 00:18:26,540
system that's been installed inside.

354
00:18:26,541 --> 00:18:29,460
And that allows the dummy to be free-flying and not

355
00:18:29,461 --> 00:18:31,580
be tethered to a computer on the ground

356
00:18:31,581 --> 00:18:35,860
and still measure data from lots of sensors.

357
00:18:35,861 --> 00:18:38,660
Data recorded by these sensors in this crash

358
00:18:38,661 --> 00:18:41,160
will allow Exponent to recreate the physical crash

359
00:18:41,161 --> 00:18:43,460
test in the lab.

360
00:18:43,461 --> 00:18:46,060
We're going to take the dummy we used in the crash test

361
00:18:46,061 --> 00:18:49,680
with the car, put it on this bike, which is tethered to the sled

362
00:18:49,681 --> 00:18:50,840
system here.

363
00:18:50,841 --> 00:18:54,220
So the idea here is to replicate the motion and the impact

364
00:18:54,221 --> 00:18:57,600
forces that the motorcycle saw hitting the car here

365
00:18:57,601 --> 00:19:00,900
on the sled so that we can study how the motorcycle works over

366
00:19:00,901 --> 00:19:04,780
and over again without breaking motorcycles all the time.

367
00:19:04,781 --> 00:19:07,600
The 9,000-pound sled will accelerate up to 30

368
00:19:07,601 --> 00:19:11,320
miles per hour in 150 feet.

369
00:19:11,321 --> 00:19:13,480
Filming the event with high-speed cameras

370
00:19:13,481 --> 00:19:17,720
requires 300 kilowatts of light, enough electricity

371
00:19:17,721 --> 00:19:21,560
to power 100 average homes.

372
00:19:21,561 --> 00:19:23,860
The Exponent team launches the sled.

373
00:19:23,861 --> 00:19:27,060
3, 2, 1, start.

374
00:19:33,080 --> 00:19:36,220
The slow motion cameras reveal that the replication of the crash

375
00:19:36,221 --> 00:19:39,020
test was a success.

376
00:19:39,021 --> 00:19:40,360
What we're seeing in the sled test

377
00:19:40,361 --> 00:19:43,260
is the sled stopping as if it was hitting the car,

378
00:19:43,261 --> 00:19:45,160
the motorcycle moving in the same manner

379
00:19:45,161 --> 00:19:47,160
as if it hit the car, and the dummy coming off

380
00:19:47,161 --> 00:19:49,460
of the motorcycle in the same manner.

381
00:19:49,461 --> 00:19:51,260
We just take a motorcycle on a sled,

382
00:19:51,261 --> 00:19:53,860
run it over and over again, looking at different components,

383
00:19:53,861 --> 00:19:59,080
only consuming one motorcycle, not dozens of them.

384
00:19:59,081 --> 00:20:01,580
Crash tests helped researchers adapt one of the car

385
00:20:01,581 --> 00:20:03,940
industry's most successful safety technologies

386
00:20:03,941 --> 00:20:06,380
to motorcycles, the airbag.

387
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:11,820
Honda has developed a fuel tank-mounted airbag

388
00:20:11,821 --> 00:20:15,120
for its Goldwing model that takes just 0.15 seconds

389
00:20:15,121 --> 00:20:17,920
to deploy.

390
00:20:17,921 --> 00:20:20,500
Upon impact, sensors in the front fork

391
00:20:20,501 --> 00:20:22,660
detect a velocity change.

392
00:20:22,661 --> 00:20:25,100
Nitrogen gas releases into the airbag,

393
00:20:25,101 --> 00:20:27,380
which absorbs the forward energy of the rider,

394
00:20:27,381 --> 00:20:29,940
reducing injuries.

395
00:20:29,941 --> 00:20:32,520
If an airbag on your bike isn't enough,

396
00:20:32,521 --> 00:20:33,780
how about an airbag jacket?

397
00:20:33,781 --> 00:20:39,780
When a rider is thrown from a cycle, a sensor activates a CO2

398
00:20:39,781 --> 00:20:43,780
cartridge, inflating the jacket to provide neck and upper body

399
00:20:43,781 --> 00:20:45,780
protection.

400
00:20:45,781 --> 00:20:49,780
Several brands of airbag jackets are already on the market.

401
00:20:49,781 --> 00:20:54,780
Other safety breakthroughs, like riding trainers, allow

402
00:20:54,781 --> 00:20:58,780
motorcyclists to gain defensive driving experience without even

403
00:20:58,781 --> 00:21:00,780
venturing on the road.

404
00:21:00,781 --> 00:21:02,780
Turn left at the next traffic light.

405
00:21:02,781 --> 00:21:05,140
It functions just like a basic motorcycle.

406
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:09,420
You have a clutch that operates, mirrors, throttle, brake,

407
00:21:09,820 --> 00:21:11,440
and your gear shifter.

408
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:14,800
It gives you realistic situations.

409
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:16,380
Things do pop out at you.

410
00:21:16,460 --> 00:21:19,380
You really have to be a defensive rider on a motorcycle.

411
00:21:20,180 --> 00:21:22,060
And you have to always be aware of your surroundings.

412
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:28,280
Considering the rider's vulnerability,

413
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:31,700
motorcycle experts agree that one safety factor

414
00:21:31,701 --> 00:21:34,500
is more important than all others.

415
00:21:34,600 --> 00:21:36,640
I would say that the helmet is the most important,

416
00:21:36,740 --> 00:21:38,540
and that's because I think the brain

417
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:40,540
is the most important part of your body.

418
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:43,840
Inside the helmet, we have energy absorbing material,

419
00:21:43,940 --> 00:21:47,640
like a foam, as well as a abrasion resistant surface

420
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:49,160
that goes over that.

421
00:21:49,260 --> 00:21:51,760
And this energy absorbing material

422
00:21:51,860 --> 00:21:55,360
will crush when the head impacts a hard surface,

423
00:21:55,460 --> 00:21:59,460
and that will absorb energy and reduce the acceleration

424
00:21:59,461 --> 00:22:03,300
that is seen by the skull and the brain inside.

425
00:22:03,400 --> 00:22:05,200
Basically, things you want to look for in a helmet

426
00:22:05,300 --> 00:22:09,300
is you want to make sure that they have minimum DOT

427
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:11,400
approval, which is Department of Transportation.

428
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:13,400
Next thing you want to look for is Snell approval,

429
00:22:13,500 --> 00:22:16,000
which is an impact rating.

430
00:22:16,100 --> 00:22:18,820
To receive a certification from the not-for-profit Snell

431
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:22,220
Memorial Foundation, helmets undergo a rigorous battery

432
00:22:22,320 --> 00:22:27,660
of tests, including impact resistance, face shield

433
00:22:27,661 --> 00:22:33,900
strength and chin guard deflection.

434
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:38,200
It's the most important safety item you can wear.

435
00:22:38,300 --> 00:22:39,900
It's, you know, it comes out.

436
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,600
How valuable is your head?

437
00:22:42,700 --> 00:22:45,580
Even at modest speeds, the impact of a motorcycle

438
00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:48,920
collision can be devastating.

439
00:22:49,020 --> 00:22:53,320
Now, imagine a skydiver on a crash course with solid ground

440
00:22:53,420 --> 00:22:57,020
after experiencing parachute failure.

441
00:22:57,021 --> 00:22:59,680
Amazingly, some have lived to tell their tale.

442
00:23:04,060 --> 00:23:08,060
Motorcyclists over 40 represent half of all US riders,

443
00:23:08,160 --> 00:23:11,860
and also half of all cycle crash fatalities.

444
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:14,180
Crashes will return on Modern Marvels.

445
00:23:14,181 --> 00:23:15,680
Marvels.

446
00:23:25,620 --> 00:23:30,160
Free falling from 10,000 feet.

447
00:23:30,260 --> 00:23:33,200
Skydivers trust both their training and equipment

448
00:23:33,300 --> 00:23:35,500
to get them safely back to Earth.

449
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:38,500
But sometimes things can go terribly wrong.

450
00:23:38,600 --> 00:23:39,880
Hi, Shanna.

451
00:23:39,980 --> 00:23:40,700
All right, Shanna.

452
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:41,800
What are you getting ready to do?

453
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:42,680
My first AFX.

454
00:23:42,780 --> 00:23:43,480
All righty then.

455
00:23:43,580 --> 00:23:44,280
Good job.

456
00:23:44,281 --> 00:23:48,280
On October 9, 2005, 21-year-old skydiver Shana Richardson

457
00:23:48,380 --> 00:23:50,780
jettisoned her main parachute and experienced

458
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:53,820
a malfunction on her reserve parachute.

459
00:23:53,920 --> 00:23:56,620
She hurtled to earth and crashed face first

460
00:23:56,720 --> 00:23:59,420
onto an asphalt parking lot.

461
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:02,440
Remarkably, she survived.

462
00:24:02,540 --> 00:24:05,540
I broke just about every bone in my face.

463
00:24:05,640 --> 00:24:07,740
The doctors describe it as eggshell fractures.

464
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:10,480
Broke all the bones in my cheekbones

465
00:24:10,580 --> 00:24:12,840
and in my nose and my eye sockets.

466
00:24:12,841 --> 00:24:15,740
I knocked out the top front five teeth in my mouth.

467
00:24:16,140 --> 00:24:17,980
I broke my pelvis in three places,

468
00:24:18,100 --> 00:24:20,340
and I had broken my leg as well.

469
00:24:22,220 --> 00:24:23,440
I should have died.

470
00:24:23,560 --> 00:24:24,500
I shouldn't be alive.

471
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:28,460
Worldwide, approximately 60 people die

472
00:24:28,461 --> 00:24:29,740
while skydiving annually.

473
00:24:31,160 --> 00:24:33,120
A third of those fatalities result

474
00:24:33,121 --> 00:24:34,920
from failure of the canopy to deploy.

475
00:24:36,140 --> 00:24:38,280
That's what happened with Shana's reserve chute.

476
00:24:39,920 --> 00:24:42,780
The dynamics of parachute failure can be complex.

477
00:24:42,840 --> 00:24:45,840
involving everything from equipment malfunctions, weather

478
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:49,180
conditions, and most frequently, operator error.

479
00:24:53,020 --> 00:24:56,420
Bob Feisty Feistamil is a US Parachuting Association

480
00:24:56,520 --> 00:25:00,260
certified instructor, master rigger, and world champion

481
00:25:00,360 --> 00:25:02,320
skydiver.

482
00:25:02,420 --> 00:25:05,560
He's performed mishap analysis on past skydiving

483
00:25:05,660 --> 00:25:07,660
fatalities.

484
00:25:07,760 --> 00:25:10,460
The main reason that people would have malfunctions,

485
00:25:10,461 --> 00:25:12,260
Usually it's a human packing error.

486
00:25:12,360 --> 00:25:14,060
If you don't have a good parachute packed,

487
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:16,600
now you have to get rid of it eventually

488
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:17,900
and go to your reserve.

489
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,600
Nobody really likes to go to their reserve for any reason.

490
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:26,980
A perceived malfunction can be just as dangerous

491
00:25:27,080 --> 00:25:29,740
as a real one, especially for a novice skydiver

492
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:32,480
like Shana West.

493
00:25:32,580 --> 00:25:34,980
It was just a beautiful sunny day,

494
00:25:35,080 --> 00:25:37,620
kind of abnormal weather for October.

495
00:25:37,720 --> 00:25:40,460
The plane ride to altitude takes about 20 miles

496
00:25:40,461 --> 00:25:44,160
and it was pretty standard as compared to all my other dives.

497
00:25:44,260 --> 00:25:47,560
I was comfortable with what I was getting ready to do.

498
00:25:47,660 --> 00:25:51,200
Her instructor, Rick West, at the time also her boyfriend,

499
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:54,100
videotaped Shana's jump so she could review her form

500
00:25:54,200 --> 00:25:56,440
following the skydive.

501
00:25:56,540 --> 00:25:58,120
Then when we got to 10,000 feet,

502
00:25:58,220 --> 00:26:01,780
it was time to leave the airplane and do the jump.

503
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:03,720
And Rick climbed out first, and he

504
00:26:03,820 --> 00:26:06,260
was standing on the side of the airplane waiting for me,

505
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:07,720
and he helped me out of the plane.

506
00:26:07,721 --> 00:26:11,920
and then we let off at the same time,

507
00:26:12,020 --> 00:26:16,360
and we free fell from 10,000 down to 5,000 feet.

508
00:26:16,460 --> 00:26:20,460
She waved off and deployed, and everything looked really good.

509
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:21,960
I was saying, good job.

510
00:26:22,060 --> 00:26:23,960
That's my girl.

511
00:26:24,060 --> 00:26:26,880
But everything wasn't good.

512
00:26:26,980 --> 00:26:28,280
Doing some practice maneuvers,

513
00:26:28,380 --> 00:26:30,680
I lost control of the parachute.

514
00:26:30,780 --> 00:26:32,380
I heard a loud snap.

515
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:36,180
And I thought, in my unexperienced mind,

516
00:26:36,181 --> 00:26:37,980
that I had broken lines.

517
00:26:38,020 --> 00:26:39,280
You can't fix broken lines.

518
00:26:39,320 --> 00:26:42,360
Your only option is to get rid of it.

519
00:26:42,380 --> 00:26:44,420
And it's not a malfunction,

520
00:26:44,460 --> 00:26:46,660
but they perceive it as a malfunction.

521
00:26:46,700 --> 00:26:48,660
And then they go to the next step,

522
00:26:48,700 --> 00:26:50,960
which is getting ready to their main parachute

523
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:53,400
and then opening up their reserve parachute.

524
00:26:53,440 --> 00:26:56,540
Shana spun downward completely out of control,

525
00:26:56,560 --> 00:26:59,700
forcing her to jettison her main chute.

526
00:26:59,740 --> 00:27:02,880
And I really quickly deployed my reserve parachute,

527
00:27:02,900 --> 00:27:05,640
and it came out, and it was just a flat,

528
00:27:05,641 --> 00:27:07,140
It straight up malfunctioned.

529
00:27:07,141 --> 00:27:09,140
It just did not do what it was supposed to do.

530
00:27:09,141 --> 00:27:10,940
Shayna!

531
00:27:10,941 --> 00:27:12,440
Shayna!

532
00:27:12,441 --> 00:27:13,840
Pop the brakes!

533
00:27:13,841 --> 00:27:16,980
It came out with a hunch slider and a tension knot,

534
00:27:16,981 --> 00:27:20,780
which prevented my parachute from opening all the way.

535
00:27:20,781 --> 00:27:23,400
Upon abandoning her main parachute,

536
00:27:23,401 --> 00:27:26,400
Shayna's reserve parachute automatically deployed.

537
00:27:26,401 --> 00:27:29,300
But because Shayna was in a spin at the time,

538
00:27:29,301 --> 00:27:31,900
the lines of her reserve chute became tangled,

539
00:27:31,901 --> 00:27:34,640
creating a tension knot.

540
00:27:34,641 --> 00:27:36,680
The knot prevented the slider on her reserve chute

541
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:38,880
from descending.

542
00:27:38,980 --> 00:27:41,220
The slider's normal function is to slow the opening

543
00:27:41,320 --> 00:27:42,320
of the canopy.

544
00:27:42,420 --> 00:27:44,220
But in this case, the stuck slider

545
00:27:44,320 --> 00:27:48,360
prevented the reserve chute from fully opening.

546
00:27:48,460 --> 00:27:50,020
Instead of having a square parachute,

547
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:52,720
I had a triangle-shaped parachute

548
00:27:52,820 --> 00:27:56,160
cupped the wind, caused me to spin.

549
00:27:56,260 --> 00:27:57,800
And I'm thinking that she was in trouble,

550
00:27:57,900 --> 00:28:00,800
that she wasn't doing fun 360s.

551
00:28:00,900 --> 00:28:02,140
And that's when I start yelling at her.

552
00:28:04,240 --> 00:28:09,580
Well, I was pumping the brakes.

553
00:28:09,680 --> 00:28:12,280
I'm spinning out of control, and I see my problem,

554
00:28:12,380 --> 00:28:14,380
but it's just so far out of my reach

555
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:16,280
I couldn't do anything about it.

556
00:28:16,380 --> 00:28:18,080
At a certain point, you have to give up.

557
00:28:18,180 --> 00:28:19,500
There was nothing else I could do.

558
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:22,200
I had fought it until I couldn't fight it anymore,

559
00:28:22,300 --> 00:28:25,700
and it was just time to face the reality that I was going to hit.

560
00:28:25,800 --> 00:28:29,740
And in my mind, I was sure it was going to be it.

561
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:32,140
You know, I was certain I was going to die from that impact.

562
00:28:32,141 --> 00:28:35,040
But the chance of surviving an uncontrolled free fall

563
00:28:35,140 --> 00:28:39,940
from over 10,000 feet while remote is not impossible.

564
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:43,280
Perhaps the most famous incident occurred during World War II,

565
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:46,780
when Nick Alcimedi, a Royal Air Force tailcutter,

566
00:28:46,880 --> 00:28:49,460
leapt from his burning plane without a parachute

567
00:28:49,560 --> 00:28:52,260
and fell 18,000 feet.

568
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:54,260
Pine trees and a thin layer of snow

569
00:28:54,360 --> 00:28:56,840
were all that broke his fall when he landed safely

570
00:28:56,940 --> 00:28:59,260
behind enemy lines.

571
00:28:59,261 --> 00:29:04,060
To absorb shock upon impact, the US Navy encourages a knees-bent

572
00:29:04,061 --> 00:29:07,560
landing position and collapsing in the following order, feet,

573
00:29:07,561 --> 00:29:10,500
knees, buttocks, then shoulder.

574
00:29:10,501 --> 00:29:14,000
But can a skydiver actually increase his or her chances of

575
00:29:14,001 --> 00:29:17,520
survival in an uncontrolled freefall?

576
00:29:17,521 --> 00:29:21,420
In a fall from 15,000 feet with the correct head-downward body

577
00:29:21,421 --> 00:29:25,220
attitude, the subject could control his lateral trajectory

578
00:29:25,221 --> 00:29:29,260
by as much as two miles.

579
00:29:29,261 --> 00:29:30,920
this could provide the opportunity

580
00:29:30,921 --> 00:29:34,160
to select the best landing site.

581
00:29:34,161 --> 00:29:37,740
In Shana West's case, she had no control over her fall

582
00:29:37,741 --> 00:29:41,340
and was virtually parallel to the ground when she hit.

583
00:29:41,341 --> 00:29:43,080
If I hadn't have been spinning so fast,

584
00:29:43,081 --> 00:29:45,640
and actually, see, at the very end, I lost consciousness.

585
00:29:45,641 --> 00:29:47,940
So I landed belly to earth.

586
00:29:47,941 --> 00:29:50,120
It dispersed the impact throughout my body

587
00:29:50,121 --> 00:29:52,020
instead of putting it all in one spot.

588
00:29:52,021 --> 00:29:52,920
Shana!

589
00:29:56,220 --> 00:29:57,880
Damn it!

590
00:29:57,881 --> 00:30:03,220
She spun it all the way down and impacted belly of earth

591
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:06,680
in a parking lot, and it was just a big pop.

592
00:30:06,780 --> 00:30:11,020
Shana's speed upon impact, 50 miles per hour.

593
00:30:11,120 --> 00:30:13,120
I was very confused as to where I was,

594
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:16,040
what had happened, and why I was still alive,

595
00:30:16,140 --> 00:30:19,040
because I truly had expected to die when I hit.

596
00:30:19,140 --> 00:30:24,480
And to be alive was probably the biggest shock of it all.

597
00:30:24,580 --> 00:30:26,180
Shana was airlifted to a hospital

598
00:30:26,181 --> 00:30:29,120
in Fayetteville, Arkansas, where another big surprise

599
00:30:29,220 --> 00:30:31,120
awaited her.

600
00:30:31,220 --> 00:30:33,620
They checked me in and, of course, doing every x-ray

601
00:30:33,720 --> 00:30:37,420
and CAT scan and MRI and blood tests that they can do.

602
00:30:37,520 --> 00:30:39,820
And through all that, not only did they discover all the

603
00:30:39,920 --> 00:30:42,900
fractures that I had, but they also came back with a positive

604
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,560
pregnancy test that I was two weeks pregnant.

605
00:30:45,660 --> 00:30:48,000
They told me that they weren't sure that the baby was even

606
00:30:48,100 --> 00:30:50,240
going to make it through the extensive surgery she's going

607
00:30:50,340 --> 00:30:53,780
to have to have, a lot of the medications to knock you out,

608
00:30:53,880 --> 00:30:55,380
you know, keep you sedated.

609
00:30:55,381 --> 00:30:59,220
So Shana went through the ER, then she had, oh man,

610
00:30:59,221 --> 00:31:02,760
long, extensive surgeries, three or four in a row.

611
00:31:02,761 --> 00:31:05,320
Just about exactly nine months after the accident,

612
00:31:05,321 --> 00:31:08,760
I had a little boy, and he is perfect.

613
00:31:08,761 --> 00:31:13,560
One, two, three, jump!

614
00:31:13,561 --> 00:31:14,700
Boosh!

615
00:31:14,701 --> 00:31:15,700
All right.

616
00:31:15,701 --> 00:31:18,940
He has no lasting effects or damage.

617
00:31:18,941 --> 00:31:22,000
He is a spunky little two-year-old little boy now,

618
00:31:22,001 --> 00:31:25,240
running around with as much energy as any little boy

619
00:31:25,241 --> 00:31:27,300
I've ever seen.

620
00:31:27,301 --> 00:31:30,500
Shana no longer skydives, but she still appreciates

621
00:31:30,501 --> 00:31:33,640
the sport's appeal.

622
00:31:33,641 --> 00:31:34,940
It's an extreme sport.

623
00:31:34,941 --> 00:31:38,640
You jump out in an airplane, and there's danger there,

624
00:31:38,641 --> 00:31:42,160
but that's what we all do it for.

625
00:31:42,161 --> 00:31:43,360
You want the adrenaline.

626
00:31:43,361 --> 00:31:45,600
You want the rush.

627
00:31:45,601 --> 00:31:48,600
You respect the sport because of the danger that there is

628
00:31:48,601 --> 00:31:50,560
involved.

629
00:31:50,561 --> 00:31:53,700
A skydiver crashing to Earth can result in the death of an

630
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:55,940
individual.

631
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,960
But a single asteroid crashing to Earth could potentially

632
00:31:59,040 --> 00:32:03,900
destroy all life on the planet.

633
00:32:03,980 --> 00:32:08,880
One of every 1,000 skydivers will die while making a jump.

634
00:32:08,940 --> 00:32:11,740
Crashes will return on Modern Marvels.

635
00:32:11,741 --> 00:32:21,580
Earth is under bombardment.

636
00:32:21,581 --> 00:32:27,720
Every day, 200 tons of meteoroids, ice, and dust

637
00:32:27,721 --> 00:32:31,220
from space collide with our planet.

638
00:32:31,221 --> 00:32:34,000
Lucky for us that most of it disintegrates during entry

639
00:32:34,001 --> 00:32:35,560
into our atmosphere.

640
00:32:35,561 --> 00:32:39,060
Some, however, can't be stopped.

641
00:32:39,061 --> 00:32:44,060
In the fall of 1992, a meteoroid put on a spectacular display

642
00:32:44,160 --> 00:32:49,200
as it shattered upon entry into the Earth's atmosphere.

643
00:32:49,300 --> 00:32:52,740
A 27-pound piece of the meteorite struck a parked car

644
00:32:52,840 --> 00:32:56,240
in Peekskill, New York.

645
00:32:56,340 --> 00:32:59,140
A meteoroid is larger than dust but smaller

646
00:32:59,240 --> 00:33:01,180
than an asteroid.

647
00:33:01,280 --> 00:33:04,760
An asteroid is at least 150 feet in diameter

648
00:33:04,860 --> 00:33:09,060
but can be as large as a small planet.

649
00:33:09,061 --> 00:33:12,760
Comets, which can range in size from a few feet to tens of miles

650
00:33:12,761 --> 00:33:16,560
in diameter, are often described as dirty snowballs,

651
00:33:16,561 --> 00:33:20,140
since they are composed of ice, rock, and dust.

652
00:33:20,141 --> 00:33:24,640
They're also distinguished by a glowing tail.

653
00:33:24,641 --> 00:33:26,940
There are nearly 1,000 known asteroids

654
00:33:26,941 --> 00:33:29,820
that cross the Earth's path, which are at least a kilometer

655
00:33:29,821 --> 00:33:32,980
wide.

656
00:33:32,981 --> 00:33:35,860
When large meteoroids, asteroids, or comets penetrate

657
00:33:35,861 --> 00:33:40,360
our atmospheric shield, the results can be catastrophic.

658
00:33:40,460 --> 00:33:45,140
50,000 years ago, a 150-foot diameter meteor crashed to Earth

659
00:33:45,240 --> 00:33:48,900
in the Arizona desert, creating a crater half a mile in diameter

660
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:52,180
and 570 feet deep.

661
00:33:52,280 --> 00:33:54,580
The projectile that hit that meteor crater

662
00:33:54,680 --> 00:33:57,080
is now thought to have arrived at the Earth

663
00:33:57,180 --> 00:33:59,520
at a relatively low speed, and that

664
00:33:59,620 --> 00:34:03,920
resulted in an explosion that's roughly a couple of megaton

665
00:34:04,020 --> 00:34:05,860
equivalent TNT.

666
00:34:05,861 --> 00:34:10,660
That's 150 times the magnitude of the Hiroshima atomic bomb,

667
00:34:10,760 --> 00:34:14,460
powerful enough to excavate 175 million tons of rock.

668
00:34:17,300 --> 00:34:20,500
But that collision is dwarfed by the six-mile-wide asteroid

669
00:34:20,600 --> 00:34:24,620
that crashed to Earth 65 million years ago in Chicxulub, Mexico,

670
00:34:24,720 --> 00:34:26,220
off the Yucatan Peninsula.

671
00:34:30,160 --> 00:34:32,220
The debris it scattered into the atmosphere

672
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:34,960
created an impact winter, blocking out

673
00:34:34,961 --> 00:34:39,440
blocking out the sun for months and disrupting the food chain.

674
00:34:39,540 --> 00:34:40,960
With that much debris in the atmosphere,

675
00:34:41,060 --> 00:34:43,940
you would expect the entire ecosystem of the Earth

676
00:34:44,040 --> 00:34:46,000
to be affected.

677
00:34:46,100 --> 00:34:49,640
80% of all animal species, including the dinosaurs,

678
00:34:49,740 --> 00:34:52,140
became extinct.

679
00:34:52,240 --> 00:34:54,740
Our understanding of mega crashes with space objects

680
00:34:54,840 --> 00:34:58,880
took a giant step forward in 1994.

681
00:34:58,980 --> 00:35:02,380
Observation of impact plumes from comet Shoemaker-Levy 9's

682
00:35:02,381 --> 00:35:06,860
with Jupiter sent shockwaves through the astronomy community.

683
00:35:06,960 --> 00:35:09,860
This was, of course, very exciting to scientists,

684
00:35:09,960 --> 00:35:12,860
first because of the ability to predict and then observe

685
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:16,720
such a phenomenally large event, second because people

686
00:35:16,820 --> 00:35:19,960
became a lot more concerned about that possibility here.

687
00:35:20,060 --> 00:35:23,400
If Shoemaker-Levy 9 comet had collided with Earth,

688
00:35:23,500 --> 00:35:26,780
it would have wiped out all human life.

689
00:35:26,880 --> 00:35:29,100
It's no coincidence that three days following

690
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:31,840
the comet's impact, Congress authorized

691
00:35:31,841 --> 00:35:35,480
NASA to create a system to map and track asteroids and comets

692
00:35:35,580 --> 00:35:37,980
that could threaten our planet.

693
00:35:38,080 --> 00:35:40,980
NASA's solution was NEAT.

694
00:35:41,080 --> 00:35:44,180
NEAT stands for Near Earth Asteroid Tracking.

695
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:47,180
It's a program run by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory here

696
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:49,600
at Palomar in Hawaii, where basically they're

697
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:52,960
looking for asteroids that are going to come close to the Earth

698
00:35:53,060 --> 00:35:54,060
at some point.

699
00:35:54,160 --> 00:35:56,600
Currently, the NEAT program operates from here at Palomar

700
00:35:56,700 --> 00:36:00,100
by using a 48-inch telescope that robotically searches

701
00:36:00,101 --> 00:36:02,100
searches through the sky during the night

702
00:36:02,200 --> 00:36:04,000
and photographs different regions,

703
00:36:04,100 --> 00:36:07,840
re-photographs them, and looks for moving targets.

704
00:36:07,940 --> 00:36:09,840
Although Palomar Observatory is equipped

705
00:36:09,940 --> 00:36:13,040
with a giant 200-inch Hale telescope,

706
00:36:13,140 --> 00:36:15,340
its 48-inch Schmidt Wide Angle Telescope

707
00:36:15,440 --> 00:36:17,260
is better suited to searching for asteroids.

708
00:36:20,260 --> 00:36:22,560
When you hunt for asteroids, you don't know exactly

709
00:36:22,660 --> 00:36:23,660
where they are.

710
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:26,100
And so having a wide-angle telescope,

711
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:27,960
that's the perfect tool for the job

712
00:36:27,961 --> 00:36:32,340
because you can see so much at one time that if you use a giant

713
00:36:32,341 --> 00:36:34,840
telescope, you have a narrow field of view,

714
00:36:34,841 --> 00:36:38,940
and it would take much longer to hunt through that same region

715
00:36:38,941 --> 00:36:41,640
to actually see anything.

716
00:36:41,641 --> 00:36:44,780
The Schmidt telescope gathers light from the night sky

717
00:36:44,781 --> 00:36:50,960
and focuses it on a 160 megapixel electronic camera.

718
00:36:50,961 --> 00:36:56,480
Each night, the neat camera takes 20,000 to 30,000 pictures.

719
00:36:56,481 --> 00:36:59,320
A microwave relay system sends images to the Jet Propulsion

720
00:36:59,420 --> 00:37:03,880
Laboratory 150 miles away.

721
00:37:03,980 --> 00:37:08,220
Computers at JPL analyze these data, sorting out those images

722
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:11,820
that suggest comets or asteroids.

723
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:15,940
NEAT takes three pictures of the night sky separated by 30

724
00:37:16,040 --> 00:37:17,640
minutes of time.

725
00:37:17,740 --> 00:37:20,780
The software looks for moving objects

726
00:37:20,880 --> 00:37:24,880
against the background stars.

727
00:37:24,881 --> 00:37:29,020
NEET's mission is to identify 90% of all comets and asteroids

728
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:31,720
that cross the Earth's orbit, which are over 1 kilometer

729
00:37:31,820 --> 00:37:33,620
in diameter.

730
00:37:33,720 --> 00:37:37,460
If a 1 kilometer asteroid hit a major urban area,

731
00:37:37,560 --> 00:37:41,500
the destruction would be cataclysmic.

732
00:37:41,600 --> 00:37:44,260
But what can we do if NEET locates an object on a collision

733
00:37:44,360 --> 00:37:46,700
course with Earth?

734
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:48,940
If we suddenly found that there was an asteroid tonight that

735
00:37:49,040 --> 00:37:52,180
was going to hit the Earth, there's nothing we can do.

736
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:54,880
But if you have years' notice and you can convince them

737
00:37:54,881 --> 00:37:56,880
to actually do something about it,

738
00:37:56,881 --> 00:38:00,880
then you certainly have the opportunity to save the Earth.

739
00:38:00,881 --> 00:38:03,520
Probably the worst thing to do to nudge an asteroid

740
00:38:03,521 --> 00:38:05,220
is to blow it up like in the movies,

741
00:38:05,221 --> 00:38:06,720
because you're going to create more pieces,

742
00:38:06,721 --> 00:38:08,220
and they're going to go everywhere,

743
00:38:08,221 --> 00:38:09,920
and those are definitely going to hit the Earth.

744
00:38:09,921 --> 00:38:12,640
But if you can get a spacecraft there,

745
00:38:12,641 --> 00:38:17,040
you can actually change the path, the orbit of that asteroid.

746
00:38:17,041 --> 00:38:21,040
We may be in for a close call in 2029.

747
00:38:21,041 --> 00:38:25,040
On Friday the 13th in April of 2029,

748
00:38:25,080 --> 00:38:28,140
there will be an asteroid that will come so close to the Earth

749
00:38:28,180 --> 00:38:30,120
that if you're in Europe that night,

750
00:38:30,140 --> 00:38:34,080
you'll actually be able to watch it go across the skies.

751
00:38:34,120 --> 00:38:37,160
It'll come closer to the Earth than geostationary satellites,

752
00:38:37,180 --> 00:38:38,460
but it will miss.

753
00:38:38,500 --> 00:38:39,920
We know this for sure.

754
00:38:39,960 --> 00:38:43,320
There is some uncertainty as to its exact path, however,

755
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:47,100
and if it falls within a very specific path,

756
00:38:47,140 --> 00:38:51,040
seven years later, it will return and hit the Earth.

757
00:38:51,041 --> 00:38:55,240
That impact could result in as many as 10 million casualties,

758
00:38:55,241 --> 00:38:58,880
but scientists estimate the odds for such a collision are 1

759
00:38:58,881 --> 00:39:04,320
in 45,000. Still, by 2036, let's hope we

760
00:39:04,321 --> 00:39:07,360
have a deflection plan ready to go.

761
00:39:10,960 --> 00:39:14,400
While many scientists look skyward for answers, there's one

762
00:39:14,401 --> 00:39:17,600
group that's looking inward, crashing together subatomic

763
00:39:17,601 --> 00:39:20,940
subatomic particles in hopes of unlocking mysteries of the

764
00:39:20,941 --> 00:39:25,140
universe, but some fear they may trigger doomsday in the process.

765
00:39:28,140 --> 00:39:32,060
In 1996, an asteroid almost a third of a mile wide passed

766
00:39:32,061 --> 00:39:36,560
within 280,000 miles of Earth, the largest observed object to

767
00:39:36,561 --> 00:39:39,560
come that close. It was discovered only four days

768
00:39:39,561 --> 00:39:42,640
before its close encounter with our planet.

769
00:39:42,641 --> 00:39:46,640
Crashes will return on Modern Marvels.

770
00:39:47,600 --> 00:39:51,600
What are matter and energy?

771
00:39:51,601 --> 00:39:55,600
What are space and time?

772
00:39:55,601 --> 00:39:59,600
Tiny crashes may provide the answers.

773
00:39:59,601 --> 00:40:04,600
30 miles west of Chicago, at Fermilab, a 7,000 acre research

774
00:40:04,601 --> 00:40:08,600
facility, scientists smashed together subatomic particles

775
00:40:08,601 --> 00:40:12,600
and the world's largest and most complex machine.

776
00:40:12,601 --> 00:40:16,600
The Tevatron Collider is the highest energy collider

777
00:40:16,601 --> 00:40:20,640
collider in the world. Its basic purpose is to study the

778
00:40:20,641 --> 00:40:24,500
fundamental laws of physics, how the universe is put together.

779
00:40:24,501 --> 00:40:29,140
Fermilab needs so much land area because the accelerator itself

780
00:40:29,141 --> 00:40:33,380
is quite large. The main accelerator, the Tevatron,

781
00:40:33,381 --> 00:40:36,420
is approximately 4 miles in circumference.

782
00:40:36,421 --> 00:40:40,160
And then there are other accelerators that also take

783
00:40:40,161 --> 00:40:44,000
lots of space. What the Tevatron is

784
00:40:44,001 --> 00:40:48,700
accelerating are trillions of tiny protons and antiprotons,

785
00:40:48,701 --> 00:40:51,800
which travel in opposite directions.

786
00:40:51,801 --> 00:40:55,140
Protons, along with neutrons, are found inside the nuclei

787
00:40:55,141 --> 00:40:58,940
of atoms. Crashing protons into a nickel

788
00:40:58,941 --> 00:41:04,520
target at extremely high speeds produces antiprotons.

789
00:41:04,521 --> 00:41:07,020
This is the Cockroft-Walton Accelerator.

790
00:41:07,021 --> 00:41:10,000
It's where the protons begin their journey on their way

791
00:41:10,001 --> 00:41:12,260
through the Fermilab Accelerator Complex.

792
00:41:12,261 --> 00:41:16,660
because you're starting them out at rest, and the way you get

793
00:41:16,760 --> 00:41:20,260
them started is just by applying a very large voltage.

794
00:41:20,360 --> 00:41:25,200
The energy of this machine is 750,000 volts.

795
00:41:25,300 --> 00:41:27,600
In tunnels 30 feet below ground,

796
00:41:27,700 --> 00:41:30,820
the particles build up speed as they travel in opposite

797
00:41:30,920 --> 00:41:33,920
directions around the four-mile course of the Tevatron.

798
00:41:34,020 --> 00:41:36,120
Each time a particle makes the loop,

799
00:41:36,220 --> 00:41:39,660
it's given another little kick by a radio frequency field,

800
00:41:39,661 --> 00:41:43,500
building up momentum until it's making nearly 50,000 trips

801
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:47,900
around the track per second and approaches the speed of light.

802
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:50,300
Powerful magnets line the underground tunnels

803
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:54,400
to bend and focus the particles on their course.

804
00:41:54,500 --> 00:41:56,880
Once the particles are fully accelerated,

805
00:41:56,980 --> 00:42:00,020
they're allowed to collide.

806
00:42:00,120 --> 00:42:02,820
A massive detector, or particle camera,

807
00:42:02,920 --> 00:42:04,420
records the millions of collisions

808
00:42:04,520 --> 00:42:06,820
that occur every second.

809
00:42:06,821 --> 00:42:11,120
It looks at the particles that come out from this collision.

810
00:42:11,220 --> 00:42:13,460
They leave traces of themselves behind.

811
00:42:13,560 --> 00:42:16,700
And then what you get out is for each individual time

812
00:42:16,800 --> 00:42:19,940
that a proton and an antiproton smash into each other,

813
00:42:20,040 --> 00:42:23,200
we create a very detailed picture about what happened

814
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:25,380
in that one collision.

815
00:42:25,480 --> 00:42:27,440
Scientists analyze these data,

816
00:42:27,540 --> 00:42:30,440
searching for dark matter, other dimensions,

817
00:42:30,540 --> 00:42:33,240
and the origin of mass.

818
00:42:33,340 --> 00:42:36,820
This is the main control room of the Fermi Lab Accelerator.

819
00:42:36,821 --> 00:42:41,160
complex from this room. Every device in the accelerator

820
00:42:41,260 --> 00:42:44,120
can be controlled. There's something like 100,000

821
00:42:44,220 --> 00:42:47,660
devices. And we run 24 hours a day,

822
00:42:47,760 --> 00:42:50,760
seven days a week. If you had to reproduce this

823
00:42:50,860 --> 00:42:54,580
facility, it would cost several billion dollars.

824
00:42:54,680 --> 00:42:58,580
Remarkably, the Tevatron is being reproduced, only on a

825
00:42:58,680 --> 00:43:02,880
much grander scale halfway around the world.

826
00:43:02,881 --> 00:43:07,420
The Large Hadron Collider, or LHC, at CERN, which is a

827
00:43:07,480 --> 00:43:10,580
European laboratory centered in Geneva, Switzerland, the actual

828
00:43:10,660 --> 00:43:14,560
accelerator is so large it goes into France a little bit.

829
00:43:14,620 --> 00:43:19,760
The LHC is about three times the size of the Tevatron,

830
00:43:19,840 --> 00:43:23,260
with magnetic fields that are about twice or more what we can

831
00:43:23,340 --> 00:43:26,540
produce here in our accelerator.

832
00:43:26,600 --> 00:43:30,140
The LHC is so powerful that some fear the collisions

833
00:43:30,141 --> 00:43:33,840
generates might actually create a black hole capable of sucking

834
00:43:33,841 --> 00:43:38,140
in our entire planet. The scientists at Fermilab,

835
00:43:38,141 --> 00:43:40,180
who have collaborated on the project,

836
00:43:40,181 --> 00:43:43,180
dismissed the concern.

837
00:43:43,181 --> 00:43:45,720
The idea that we would generate a black hole under these

838
00:43:45,721 --> 00:43:48,900
conditions that would swallow up the Earth is such a remote

839
00:43:48,901 --> 00:43:52,300
possibility that no one would take it seriously.

840
00:43:52,301 --> 00:43:55,940
The high energy collisions the LHC will produce may release

841
00:43:55,941 --> 00:44:00,140
a variety of previously theoretical particles, including

842
00:44:00,141 --> 00:44:03,940
the Higgs boson, the holy grail of subatomic physics that

843
00:44:04,040 --> 00:44:08,440
might explain why all other particles have mass.

844
00:44:08,540 --> 00:44:10,180
These barrier-shattering collisions

845
00:44:10,280 --> 00:44:13,320
are just another example of the violent release of energy

846
00:44:13,420 --> 00:44:16,560
that we call crashes.

847
00:44:16,660 --> 00:44:19,960
To paraphrase an earlier physicist, Sir Isaac Newton,

848
00:44:20,060 --> 00:44:24,340
an object in motion tends to stay in motion, unless, of course,

849
00:44:24,440 --> 00:44:26,140
something happens to get in its way.

